emise, but never cite\nan entire reading window just because all of it was visible. An oversized proof\ncannot be forwarded or audited: a citation_capacity repair must recheck the SAME\ndraft against all supplied original text and return a genuinely sufficient smaller\nproof, or a named sourced review with value=null and unresolved=[] if no such\nproof exists. Use partial/unknown only for genuine requested-scope incomplete\nevidence; use irrelevant only when the original warrants it and current repair\npermissions allow. Preserve required IDs, originals and protected counters. Never delete a necessary\npremise merely to fit a size limit, and never remove a counter to save space.\nFor an explicit summary_revision, its inherited-source retention contract takes\npriority over minimizing citations: keep every required old identity, and choose\nonly the minimal relevant NEW citations. Do not shrink an inherited proof to fit.\nEach support value asserts the WHOLE value of that variable, including set/record\nvariables. Multiple support observations are alternative proofs of the SAME complete\nvalue, not additive fragments. Combine applicable sources into one complete value\nand cite all its premises. The controller never unions sets or merges records. If the visible text\nonly establishes part of the requested value, return partial with value=null and\nexplain the supported fragment in reason. Do not claim completeness from one window.\nrequested_scopes[slot.variable] is that variable's authoritative local scope. Use its\nentity_scope and time_scope strings exactly only when the original applies; never\ntransfer another entity/event into the requested scope. A new window containing\nonly another entity/event and no applicable observation uses no_relevant_evidence\nwith observations=[] and unresolved=[]. partial/unknown requires genuine incomplete\nevidence about the requested entity/event within that scope. Required failed draft\nIDs, protected counters and old source obligations retain their existing review\nrequirements; an empty slot cannot discard or settle them. output_contract is the terminal answer's scope,\nnot the scope of every historical fact or parent. A requested parent retains its own\ndeclared scope during terminal reading and audit. Never relabel historical evidence as\ncurrent/future evidence. required_scope, when present, is only a shorthand shared by\nALL requested_scopes. Actual parents must be supplied supported bindings with\ntheir versions; reproduce sources when joining documents. Sources may be combined\nacross documents, but list ALL joint premises. A source slice is not an independent fact.\nHonor authoritative source_role: user statements, assistant suggestions, documents\nand unknown headers differ. An assistant suggestion does not establish the user's\npreference without user evidence. Observation order is not calendar event time;\ntemporal scope needs cited explicit text or authoritative time metadata, never a\ndate guessed from message order. List sources for every object/time qualifier.\nContradiction challenges existing fact route IDs in retract_ids, with cited raw text;\nit never itself creates a false binding. Emit contrary supported values separately.\nOnly same-variable known_observations[].id values are legal retraction targets.\nIf no such route exists, no retraction is available; preserve contrary evidence as\nan independently supported value or a named sourced partial/unknown observation\nwith value=null and unresolved=[]. Never invent a target or discard evidence. Other stances require retract_ids=[].\nUnknown, absence, unexamined text, anomalies without a concrete observation are not false.\nInclude every relevant counter-observation in this batch before declaring support.\nSlots include the local variables and already-bound related parents. Check new text\nfor counterevidence to those parents as well. An observation's actual parent versions\nrefer to that variable's own planned task inputs, never to itself. A checked parent\nwith no new observation may return an empty observations list.\nFor a rule variable, value is {variables,expression,constraints,rules_complete}; only\npreplanned condition demands are allowed. Instantiate thresholds ONLY from applicable\nsource text. Rules must preserve the fixed output mode; one_reason uses one_reason,\nall_failures uses failures. Conditions can use numeric observations with units and\ncomparisons, not a guessed deletion latency. Mark incomplete rule collections false.\nIf rules_completion_required is true, seek explicit applicable completeness evidence.\nWhen a sourced complete rule supersedes a known incomplete rule, cite its original\nsources, retract the superseded route with a contradiction observation, and return\nthe complete rule as a separate support observation; never silently overwrite it.\nA successful observation at one time does not establish a universal no-return rule.\nRepresent a witnessed violation as IF(violation,false,unbound_compliance), keeping\npositive compliance unknown until explicit adequate scoped evidence supports it.\nFor semantic terminal tasks return the supported final text and final_prediction in\nthis same response, using public options only here. No later Reader will run.\nWhen public options are supplied, also return semantic_answer with its exact typed\nJSON value in this same terminal response: the supported semantic value, NOT an\noption letter. Only final_prediction holds the option label. Unsupported conclusions\nhave null labels.\nRepair returns only failed observations/slots, retaining IDs; never delete an invalid\ncounter-observation. If a failed ID has conflicting duplicate observations, inspect\nALL supplied drafts and resolve that ID without discarding potential counterevidence.\nIf repair_scope supplies replacement_observations for a duplicated ID, return EACH\nreplacement ID exactly once. Those deterministic replacement IDs separate the\noriginal drafts; fix their validation errors without dropping any draft or counter.\nrepair_scope.failed includes the actual invalid observation and\nits validation feedback. Correct those drafts in place, preserving each requested ID.\nAn id=\"*\" failure authorizes repairing that variable's whole slot, including new\nunique observation IDs for genuine additional reviews; it never unfreezes rows.\nNamed failed IDs must each remain present, including an ID rejected as an\nunrequested addition in the previous response: it is now a required failed draft.\nCorrect that draft under the same ID. Preserve genuine scoped partial/unknown\nevidence. When its original is genuinely inapplicable, a sourced irrelevant review\nwith value=null may correct that same ID only within the current edit permissions.\nNever relabel protected counters or genuine supporting/partial evidence irrelevant.\nDo not delete required IDs or add other IDs unless a wildcard or replacement list permits it.\nAlready validated observations within this current batch are frozen during its\nrepairs and must not be restated or changed. Prior known_observations remain\nsubject to the legal source-grounded contradiction/retract_ids mechanism.\nIf repair_scope supplies required_counter_observations, preserve every original\ncounter's stance, cited sources, scopes, actual parents and retraction targets.\nAn envelope-format repair does not authorize reclassifying a valid counter as\nirrelevant or dropping the fact route it challenges.\nevidence_obligations are outstanding scoped issues from earlier reads, not new\nfacts. Their original sources MUST be examined even under a new query identity.\nTo discharge an issue, explicitly return its id in that variable's optional\nresolved_obligation_ids, leave no unresolved issue in the slot, and provide\nsupported/corrective/irrelevant observations reviewing its cited original sources.\nThe union of those settled observations' source_ids must cover EVERY source_ids\nentry of the offered obligation. Review coverage is not a claim that every source\nis a premise of a support: genuinely irrelevant or corrective original text may\nhave a separate sourced review. Never add unsupported citations, classify text\nautomatically, or label supporting evidence irrelevant merely to satisfy coverage.\nAn empty slot does not resolve an earlier issue. Do not assert hypothetical\nwhole-corpus exhaustiveness requirements; scoped sufficient evidence may resolve\na missing-evidence issue without having read every memory.\n\nWhen source_span_columns is present, source_spans contains row arrays. Match each\nrow position to source_span_columns to reconstruct every span object before\nreading. References to source_spans[].id or other fields mean those objects.\nThe id column holds source aliases (sN); doc_id holds the original document ID or,\nwhen source_documents is present, its metadata reference (dN), never source_ids.\n\nfailed_batch_reviews retain earlier FAILED reading drafts, their validation errors,\nand ALL original text. They are unresolved obligations, never supported facts.\nOnly entries with review_allowed=true authorize a paid scoped recovery in this\nrequest. Recheck each failed observation using its original ID; for duplicate IDs\nuse every supplied replacement_observations ID. Keep every original counter and\nits protected stance, sources, scopes, actual parents and retraction targets.\nFor citation_capacity, recheck the complete original draft and text; a genuinely\nsufficient smaller proof is allowed, dropping a necessary premise is not. For a\nwhole-value disagreement preserve each original draft's sources and apply the\nwhole-value review rules. Other failed drafts' previously visible original citations\nmust be explicitly reviewed in sourced output, either on the corrected draft or\nan additional review observation. An invalid/unknown alias may be corrected, but\na real negative source cannot be silently replaced by an unrelated positive one.\nValidated earlier observations are context, not drafts\nto overwrite. To finish a review, put its exact id in that variable's optional\nresolved_failed_batch_ids. Omission, unrelated support or an empty slot never\nresolves a failed batch. If its meaning still cannot be established, return the\nnamed sourced partial/unknown review with value=null and unresolved=[]; the\ncontroller source issue stays pending. Failed-format recovery need not settle\nthat source issue; do not invent agreement.\nEntries with review_allowed=false remain pending; changing query identity alone\ndoes not authorize retrying a failed batch or clearing its marker.\n\nWEB SOURCE CHECK: exact entity/event/relation needs originals; audit them.\nPublication/revision/effective/event dates differ; people/orgs aren't places.\nMenu/topic labels and adjacent text prove no relation.\nAnother project's dates cannot transfer without source proof.\npartial/unknown: real incomplete evidence within the requested scope.\nEmpty ordinary retrieval uses\nno_relevant_evidence. Existing obligations still require legal model review of originals.\n\nREQUIRED WEB RETRIEVAL PROTOCOL: every requested slot declares reading_status;\nBOTH statuses require unresolved=[]. no_relevant_evidence means NO NEW admissible\nobservation: observations=[], no review/resolution IDs. Optional search_gaps lists\nuntried directions, only with this status. Old facts/obligations stay unchanged in\ncontroller state; no need to repeat them. This search result is never a negative\nfact or proof of absence. Do not cite an answer-free window as unknown; continue\nthe local bridge. evidence_assessment supplies NEW original-source-cited ordinary\nobservations. A genuine incomplete statement/ambiguity/conflict requires a named\npartial/unknown row with exact source_ids, requested scope and reason; unknown\nuses value=null. Never replace it with bare slot.unresolved or a resolution claim.\nOld evidence obligations, failed drafts and counters remain mandatory. To settle\nan offered issue, use evidence_assessment, explicitly review its original text\nwith sourced support/corrective/irrelevant observations, cover EVERY required\noriginal source, and claim the offered resolution ID. Genuinely irrelevant text\nmay settle an earlier absence-only issue; never relabel real partial facts,\nsupporting text or counters irrelevant just to close them. Empty slots and\npartial/unknown observations cannot settle evidence obligations: omit their IDs\nfrom resolved_obligation_ids. Correcting a failed draft need not settle a source\nissue. Preserve every required failed draft ID. Frozen rows, including partial/\nunknown, must not be restated. Repairs may instead use currently offered\nreviewed_observation_ids for immutable sourced rows, under all common source and\nresolution checks. Terminal composition/audit use the ordinary protocol without\nreading_status/search_gaps.\n\nweb_review_checklist is a read-only projection of THIS request's existing offers,\nnot new review authority or evidence. Conditional coverage counts only currently\noffered frozen IDs IF explicitly referenced. Only if claiming settlement of an\nobligation in THIS response, review every sources_requiring_new_model_review\noriginal with genuinely sourced new reviews where permitted. Otherwise leave its\nID unclaimed and its controller issue pending, without repeating frozen rows.\nA frozen reference cannot cover another source. Only\nclaimable_failed_batch_ids may be claimed; retained blocked IDs grant no recovery.\nAny displayed examples use invented IDs/text, never this response's IDs. Their\nirrelevant stance applies only when the actual original warrants that assessment;\notherwise preserve the partial/unknown issue. empty_withdrawal_edit_scope is\nread-only field guidance when no new observation exists; it settles no issue.\n\nweb_source_review contains exact already-visible originals and read-only links to\nprior judgments/issues/current frozen offers; it adds no evidence or permission.\ndocument_scope_review pairs requested_scopes with each document's earliest visible\noriginal, which may be mid-document. card_original_source_id refers only to this\nrequest's cards[].original_source.id. Establish the requested entity identity and\nevent/relation from offered originals before transferring any attribute. Shared\nsite, partner, topic or question context alone establishes no identity bridge.\nIn an ordinary read, compare new originals with existing requested-slot bindings:\nmatch the requested entity, event/relation and scope before taking an attribute.\nAn associated workflow, version or process can describe a different event.\nknown_observations marked model_judgment are prior model assessments, not semantic\ncertification of their source entailment. Reconsider them against the originals;\ndo not assume either the old or the new statement is correct. If a real conflict\nwarrants correction, use the existing legal contradiction/retract_ids mechanism\nand separate replacement support only where this request permits it. A review\ncard itself never retracts or changes a value. During repair, retain the offered\nedit scope and immutable current-batch rows. Only offered frozen references may\nbe used; each covers its own source_ids only. Support from a new original does\nnot review an old obligated original. Claim an obligation only after warranted\nsettled review of every required original; otherwise leave it pending. Do not\ninvent agreement or call genuine partial/counter evidence irrelevant to close it.\n\n\nReturn one complete JSON object matching this schema:\n{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"audit_verdict\":{\"enum\":[\"supported\",\"unknown\",\"conflict\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"final_prediction\":{\"type\":[\"string\",\"null\"]},\"semantic_answer\":{},\"slots\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"allOf\":[{\"else\":{\"anyOf\":[{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"minItems\":1}}}],\"not\":{\"required\":[\"search_gaps\"]}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"reading_status\":{\"const\":\"no_relevant_evidence\"}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"resolved_failed_batch_ids\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"unresolved\":{\"maxItems\":0}}}}],\"oneOf\":[{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"allOf\":[{\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\"]}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"const\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\"},\"time_scope\":{\"const\":\"agreement\"}}}}],\"else\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"type\":\"null\"}}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"const\":\"support\"}}},\"properties\":{\"retract_ids\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"]}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}}}}},\"resolved_failed_batch_ids\":{\"items\":{\"enum\":[\"failed_batch_941f8e422a0527d2cfbb9361\"]}},\"variable\":{\"const\":\"time\"}}},{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"allOf\":[{\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\"]}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"const\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\"},\"time_scope\":{\"const\":\"agreement\"}}}}],\"else\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"type\":\"null\"}}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"const\":\"support\"}}},\"properties\":{\"retract_ids\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"]}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}}}}},\"resolved_failed_batch_ids\":{\"items\":{\"enum\":[\"failed_batch_66b384057daa4b8ff9e1a2ef\"]}},\"variable\":{\"const\":\"location\"}}}],\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"id\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"parent_versions\":{\"additionalProperties\":{\"type\":\"integer\"},\"type\":\"object\"},\"reason\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"retract_ids\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"source_ids\":{\"description\":\"Exact visible source_spans[].id aliases, such as s3; never document IDs or invented IDs.\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"time_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"value\":{}},\"required\":[\"id\",\"stance\",\"value\",\"source_ids\",\"entity_scope\",\"time_scope\",\"parent_versions\",\"retract_ids\",\"reason\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"type\":\"array\"},\"reading_status\":{\"enum\":[\"no_relevant_evidence\",\"evidence_assessment\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"resolved_failed_batch_ids\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"search_gaps\":{\"description\":\"Untried search directions only; permitted with empty observations and unresolved.\",\"items\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"},\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\"},\"unresolved\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"maxItems\":0,\"type\":\"array\"},\"variable\":{\"enum\":[\"time\",\"location\"],\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"variable\",\"observations\",\"unresolved\",\"reading_status\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"maxItems\":2,\"minItems\":2,\"type\":\"array\"}},\"required\":[\"slots\"],\"type\":\"object\"}"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\"web_source_review\":{\"read_only\":true,\"grants_review_authority\":false,\"existing_permissions_unchanged\":true,\"document_scope_review\":{\"requested_scopes\":{\"time\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"},\"location\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}},\"documents\":[{\"doc_id\":\"web-f2480c9be1232e0ef2ee3058\",\"earliest_visible_original\":{\"id\":\"s57\",\"doc_id\":\"web-f2480c9be1232e0ef2ee3058\",\"start\":9679,\"end\":10033,\"text\":\"The results were 22 in favour of an online meeting, and 11 in favour of a physical meeting, a two thirds majority. After further discussion with the ICSE 2019 General Chair and ICSE 2020 PC Chairs, we decided to propose a review process based on an online PB+PC meeting (Appendix A). We sent the proposal to the ICSE Steering Committee in February 2018. \",\"source_role\":\"document\",\"source_message_indices\":[],\"time_metadata\":{}}}]},\"cards\":[]},\"original_question\":\"When and where was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"task\":{\"question\":\"When and where was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"output_slot\":\"agreement_details\",\"answer_type\":\"record\",\"inputs\":[],\"execution\":\"retrieval\"},\"grounded_subquestion\":\"When and where was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"request_context\":{\"query_identity\":\"e6ad7f7e6ab9df22fd3389f9af0cd6e9559ffa610181d5bb0bcf3d36f8fb59da\",\"algorithm_version\":\"dagbt_joint_memory_v1\",\"program_identity\":\"75343b782f3b360c051fd41948e48bcf96faf4f62e0ce27acee42261136ccd68\",\"initial_dag_identity\":\"7a8c206e30afbad778ff12a979d967523991179bb94edf5c8479e76fc78b3857\"},\"variables\":{\"time\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"demand\":\"agreement_details\",\"open\":true,\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}},\"location\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"demand\":\"agreement_details\",\"open\":true,\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}}},\"requested_slots\":[\"time\",\"location\"],\"requested_scopes\":{\"time\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"},\"location\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}},\"output_contract\":{\"mode\":\"value\",\"result_type\":\"record\",\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\",\"explanation\":\"none\",\"fields\":[\"time\",\"location\"]},\"program_revision\":0,\"rule_binding\":null,\"supported_parents\":{},\"known_observations\":[],\"source_spans\":[[\"s57\",\"d1\",9679,10033,\"The results were 22 in favour of an online meeting, and 11 in favour of a physical meeting, a two thirds majority. After further discussion with the ICSE 2019 General Chair and ICSE 2020 PC Chairs, we decided to propose a review process based on an online PB+PC meeting (Appendix A). We sent the proposal to the ICSE Steering Committee in February 2018. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s58\",\"d1\",10033,10354,\"After an online discussion about the proposal among the ICSE Steering Committee members, there was a vote and the proposal was approved in March 2018 (16 votes for and 6 votes against).  2.c Selection of the PB and the PC  One can argue that selection of the PB/PC is the most significant task performed by any PC Chair. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s59\",\"d1\",10354,10679,\"Our main goal was to select experienced researchers who have demonstrated excellent performance as reviewers and discussants in prior conferences as PB members, and to select researchers with a strong track record both as researchers and reviewers as PC members. We looked for significant prior PC experience for PB members. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s60\",\"d1\",10679,10912,\"For PC members we looked for recognized expertise as demonstrated by strong publication records in quality venues, while taking into account their seniority and research area (which can lead to variations in publication records).    \",\"document\",[]],[\"s61\",\"d1\",10912,11312,\"We identified several criteria for selection of the PB/PC: ● Aim for proportional coverage of research areas taking into account the historical number of submissions for each research area (we used the data for distribution of papers to research areas from ICSE 2018)  ● Aim for a good gender balance ● Aim for geographic diversity ● Aim for industry representation ● Include the PC Chairs from ICSE \",\"document\",[]],[\"s62\",\"d1\",11312,11483,\"2018 and ICSE 2020 in either the PB or PC ● Aim to satisfy the SIGSOFT policy: For recurring events, at least one-third of the program committee membership should change. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s63\",\"d1\",11483,11805,\"Most program committee members should not serve for more than three consecutive terms and in no instance should an individual serve more than four consecutive terms.  We created a spreadsheet containing information about candidates for PB and PC (such as research areas, organization, country, gender, industry/academia). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s64\",\"d1\",11805,12094,\"We used scripts created by prior ICSE chairs for mining publications in DBLP and prior conference committees to search for overlooked candidates with strong records. We met with the PC chairs of ICSE 2018 and ICSE 2017 to get their input on past performance of PB/PC candidates. Based on \\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s65\",\"d1\",12094,12484,\"all the information we gathered and the guidelines we listed above, we created an invitation list and sent the PB invitations in December 2017 and the PC invitations in January 2018. The ICSE 2019 PB and PC were finalized by February 2018.  Of the 132 invitations that were sent out, 21 were either declined or ignored. For these, we added new names, maintaining the criteria given above.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s66\",\"d1\",12484,12812,\"In the final list, the gender balance of the PB was 16F/16M and of the PC was 39F/59M. Statistics on geographic diversity are given in Section 4. Generally, we achieved good geographic diversity, although we could not identify suitable PC/PB members from some countries (most notably India) whilst satisfying other constraints. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s67\",\"d1\",12812,13060,\"It is also instructive to compare the ratio of submitting authors to PB/PC members across countries: such an analysis reveals stark disparities. For example, the ratio for the USA is 11:1 but for China is 108:1. More is said on this in Section 4.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s68\",\"d1\",13060,13437,\"2.d Timeline and the Review Process  In order to meet the PB and PC members, prepare them for the review process, and receive their feedback about the review process, we organized two separate meetings at ICSE 2018, one for the ICSE 2019 PB and one for the ICSE 2019 PC. In these meetings, we went over a set of slides explaining the review process and asked for their input.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s69\",\"d1\",13437,13837,\"We used the same review criteria that were used in recent ICSEs: ● Soundness: How well the paper’s contributions are supported by rigorous application of appropriate research methods, ● Significance: The extent to which the paper’s contributions are novel, original, and important, with respect to the existing body of knowledge, ● Verifiability: Whether the paper includes sufficient information to \",\"document\",[]],[\"s70\",\"d1\",13837,14224,\"support independent verification or replication of the paper’s claimed contributions, ● Presentation: Whether the paper’s quality of writing meets the high standards of ICSE, including clear descriptions and explanations, adequate use of the English language, absence of major ambiguity, clearly readable figures and tables, and adherence to the formatting instructions provided below.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s71\",\"d1\",14224,14496,\"Although the above criteria were listed in the review form, reviewers were not asked to score papers separately on Significance, Soundness, Novelty, Replicability and Presentation Quality. It was considered that these questions did not add value to the decision-making.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s72\",\"d1\",14496,14874,\"Another change in the review form compared to prior years was the addition of a question where reviewers were asked to rank their set of papers - for each paper, to say whether the paper was in the Top 15%, Top 16-30%, Top 31-50%, Bottom 50% of the ICSE submissions. Since no reviewer read all submissions, reviewers were asked to use their batch of submissions as a guideline. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s73\",\"d1\",14874,15027,\"The rationale in adding this question was to get reviewers to self-reflect on any biases in their scoring and help them normalize their recommendations. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s74\",\"d1\",15027,15296,\"For example, a reviewer marking a paper as top 15% but giving a strong reject might reconsider their decision and change either their ranking or the recommendation, or might provide an explanation of why those particular scores make sense during the discussion phase.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s75\",\"d1\",15296,15534,\"Although we did not empirically evaluate the effects of this new question, in at least one instance the incongruence between the ranking and the score given by a reviewer was discussed and this had an influence on the decision making.   \\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s76\",\"d1\",15534,15934,\" The review process was based on the following principles:  After the desk rejects: ● All papers received 3 reviews written by PC members ○ More reviewers could be assigned if the expertise among the current reviewers was low ● All papers got a chance at a rebuttal of ALL the reviews ○ This implies that no reviews were conducted after the rebuttal period, or equivalently, all reviews had to be sub\",\"document\",[]],[\"s77\",\"d1\",15934,15970,\"mitted before the rebuttal period.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s78\",\"d1\",15970,16370,\"● All papers received a meta-review, written by the PB members, summarizing the discussion, the reaction to the rebuttal and the decision  ● PB members did not re-review the papers but participated in discussions either as a moderator (discussion lead) or as an additional discussant  After the initial check for violations of submission instructions (which can lead to desk rejection) we carried out\",\"document\",[]],[\"s79\",\"d1\",16370,16770,\" a three phase decision making process: ● Phase 1: After rebuttal, papers with unanimous negative or positive scores were marked as accept or reject as early as possible (we had expected ~60% of papers to be decided this way)  ● Phase 2: Papers with mixed scores were discussed further to reach a consensus decision by the reviewers, under the guidance of the PB member (we had expected ~25-30% of pa\",\"document\",[]],[\"s80\",\"d1\",16770,17060,\"pers to be decided this way) ● Phase 3: Papers for which reviewers were unable to reach a consensus were discussed during the two-day online PB/PC meeting (we had expected ~10-15% of papers to be decided this way)  A high level overview of the review process is shown in the figure below: \\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s81\",\"d1\",17060,17443,\" \\n\\nThe actual numbers for different phases of the review process were as follows: ● 529 submissions were received. ● 25 (5%) papers were desk rejected either due to double blind rule violation or due to exceeding the page limit (we applied the page limit strictly; any paper that had technical content beyond page 10 was rejected even if it was a single line beyond the page limit). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s82\",\"d1\",17443,17823,\"● 10 (2%) papers were withdrawn by the authors at various stages of the review process.  ● All remaining 494 papers received at least 3 reviews and one meta-review.   ○ 302 (57%) consensus papers were decided in Phase 1  ○ 192 papers received mixed scores and were discussed in Phase 2. For 127 of these papers (24%) PB and PC members reached a consensus decision before Phase 3. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s83\",\"d1\",17823,18172,\"○ The remaining 65 papers (12%) were discussed in Phase 3. All decisions were finalized by the end of the two-day online PC meeting.  ● 109 (21%) papers were accepted.   It is interesting to note that our predictions about how many papers would be decided in each phase (which we made based on the data from prior years) were reasonably accurate.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s84\",\"d1\",18172,18514,\"We started the online discussions as soon as the reviews were received, although we asked reviewers not to make decisions before the rebuttal period (this differs from 2018 when reviewers were asked - but not mandated - to wait for the author responses). After Phase 2, we assigned two more PB members to each undecided paper as discussants. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s85\",\"d1\",18514,18864,\"Discussant PB members were required to read the papers, all the reviews and the discussions. The role of the discussant was to add an additional perspective on a paper to aid decision-making and to take a view on unresolved issues in the reviews.  During the online meeting (Phase 3) the goal was to reach a decision within the 2 days on all papers. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s86\",\"d1\",18864,19251,\"During the online meeting discussion, each paper had at least the following participants: 3 (or more) PC reviewers, a PB discussion leader, and 2 PB discussants. Moreover, the discussions were open to any other PB/PC member with no conflict-of-interest. We empowered the PB members to finalize the decision in cases where reviewers and discussants were unable to converge to a decision. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s87\",\"d1\",19251,19540,\"In practice, consensus was always reached. The PB/PC were instructed that PC Chairs would make an executive decision if a paper remained unresolved at the end of the two-day period. This was not necessary for any papers.  During the online meeting we used Easychair for online discussion. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s88\",\"d1\",19540,19936,\"We occasionally sent emails to the entire PB and PC to answer common questions that came up during discussions. The key issue during the online meeting was to make sure that the PB and PC members were responsive and the discussions were progressing. PC chairs monitored all the ongoing discussions to make sure that progress was being made. We did not use synchronous conversations (skype etc.). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s89\",\"d1\",19936,20300,\"Time difference was a challenge but we do not think it adversely affected the review process.  The ICSE 2019 reviews that were sent to authors did not include the scores entered by the reviewers. Scores were included in the ICSE 2018 reviews sent to authors but not in prior \\nyears. Some authors raised strong objections to scores being excluded from the reviews. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s90\",\"d1\",20300,20665,\"Authors complained about the lack of scores in the reviews since they believed that having the scores helps them to write a better response to reviewers and to make a decision about whether they should withdraw the paper. We told authors that we view the reviewers' scores as part of the PB/PC discussions (like the online comments reviewers post about the paper). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s91\",\"d1\",20665,20989,\"In their response, we encouraged the authors to focus on the substance of the reviews and the explicit questions provided by the reviewers. Sending review scores has become common in conferences, so author expectations may be changing due to this. Future chairs should consider the pros and cons of sending review scores.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s92\",\"d1\",20989,21389,\"One minor issue we faced during the process was that in preparation of the meta-reviews PB members sometimes upload “dummy” reviews to indicate which direction the decision is going, to be updated later when they had time to enter a complete meta-review. In one case, a PB member forgot to update the “dummy” meta-review and important feedback summarizing the discussion was not sent to the authors. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s93\",\"d1\",21389,21788,\"We eventually noticed this and sent the information to the authors.  In another case, we received a complaint from authors of a paper that some comments in a review were discriminatory against non-native English speakers. We let the PC member know that it is not appropriate to speculate if an author is or is not a native English speaker in a review.  The PC member promptly revised the review.    \",\"document\",[]],[\"s94\",\"d1\",21788,22171,\"Given that the workload is significant for the PB and PC and the reviews and meta-reviews are written under time pressure, it is crucial for PB/PC members to make a “sanity check” pass on all their reviews and meta-reviews before they are sent to authors.  We did not make early reject decisions in ICSE 2019 other than desk reject decisions which were sent to authors in September. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s95\",\"d1\",22171,22263,\"For the remaining submissions, the notifications were sent on December 12th as announced.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s96\",\"d1\",22263,22663,\"The timeline of the review process was as follows:  TASK DATES submission deadline Aug 24 (Fri) bidding + COI Aug 25-Aug 29 (Sat-Wed) desk rejects Sep 3-4 (Mon-Tue) paper assignments Aug 30-Sep 5 (Thu-Wed) 1st round reviews Sep 26 (Wed) 2nd round reviews Oct 17 (Wed) review assessment & improvement Sep 26-Nov 7 (Wed-Wed) additional review requests Oct 18-23 (Thu-Tue) additional paper assignments O\",\"document\",[]],[\"s97\",\"d1\",22663,22818,\"ct 24-25 (Wed-Thu) additional reviews Oct 26-Nov 2 (Fri-Fri) rebuttal Nov 12-14 (Mon-Wed) online discussion Sep 26-Nov 30 (Fri) consensus accept & reject? \",\"document\",[]],[\"s98\",\"d1\",22818,23098,\"Nov 19 (Mon)  pre meeting accept & reject Nov 26 (Mon) \\nassign extra PB members as discussant Nov 26-27 (Mon-Tue) online meeting Dec 3-4 (Mon-Tue) author notification Dec 12   Note that we provided a table showing a detailed list of deadlines for both the PB and PC (Appendix B). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s99\",\"d1\",23098,23438,\"The link for this was provided as a reminder in all email communications with the PB/PC: it served as an easily accessible source of information on upcoming deadlines and helped to keep everyone on track. Informal feedback has been that this resource was very well received and so we encourage future PC Chairs to adopt a similar practice. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s100\",\"d1\",23438,23785,\"We also provided a link to a FAQ page (Appendix C), which we updated as the review process evolved.   2.e Conference Program  The General Chair and PC Co-Chairs decided to introduce a “blended” program for ICSE 2019 where papers from the Technical, SEIP, NIER, Journal First, and Demonstration tracks were combined in sessions organized by topic. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s101\",\"d1\",23785,24138,\"The program consisted of 90 or 120 minutes long blended sessions. Presentation times were allocated for different tracks as follows: ● Technical track papers: 20 minutes ● SEIP track papers: 20 minutes ● Demonstration track papers: 20 minutes ● NIER track papers: 10 minutes ● Journal first track papers: 10 minutes  These timings include time for Q&A. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s102\",\"d1\",24138,24480,\"For 20 minute presentations we recommended 15-17 minute talks followed by 1 or 2 questions from the audience. For 10 minute presentations we recommended 8 minute talks followed by 1 or 2 questions from the audience. Most sessions were followed with a 10 minute discussion period for the audience to ask more detailed questions of presenters. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s103\",\"d1\",24480,24804,\"We invited the authors to bring a poster about their work to the presentation room to facilitate discussions during the discussion period at the end of the session.   The program also designated some papers as more industrially relevant. These papers were marked in the conference program as part of the “Industry Program”. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s104\",\"d1\",24804,25110,\"The decision on which papers to include was informed by an additional question in the review form asking PC members to mark industrially-relevant papers. Due to scheduling constraints, some Industry Program sessions also included papers not so marked - based on the General Chair/PC Chairs’ best judgment. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s105\",\"d1\",25110,25397,\"One of the three ICSE keynotes was a SEIP keynote and also advertised as part of the Industry Program  2.f Awards  ACM Distinguished Paper Award Selection ACM allows ICSE to select up to 10% of accepted papers as award papers. Rounding up, this leads to up to 11 awards for ICSE 2019.  \\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s106\",\"d1\",25397,25761,\"ACM partially stipulates a process for selecting awardees, namely: “The program committee will take a weighted vote, respecting the conflict of interest rules in place for the conference, to identify the top candidates among the papers. The program committee chair(s) will use the results of the weighted votes as a primary basis for selecting the award papers.”  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s107\",\"d1\",25761,26090,\"To make decisions on award papers, we applied the following process.  1. The review form included a question asking reviewers if they wanted to nominate the paper for the award. Of the 1487 reviews we received, 42 of them nominated a paper for the award. Of these 42, only 4 received a nomination from more than one reviewer. 2. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s108\",\"d1\",26090,26385,\"We selected a longlist of 15 papers from these 42, according to the following criterion: at least one reviewer nominates the paper for an award AND there are no negative scores in the reviews.  3. We conducted a poll of the PB/PC asking them to vote for up to three papers from the longlist. 4. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s109\",\"d1\",26385,26734,\"Based on the vote and a careful reading of the reviews and online discussion, 11 papers were selected to receive an award.  There appears to be no standard process for selecting Best Paper awardees at ICSE. Rather, each year, the PC Chairs use their best judgment to decide on a process. It may be worth the ICSE SC introducing a standard process.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s110\",\"d1\",26734,27131,\"Distinguished Reviewer Awards PB and PC members are volunteers who spend a significant amount of time and effort during the review process. We believe that it is worthwhile to acknowledge the efforts of reviewers who do an outstanding job. For this purpose, we asked PB members to nominate PC members for the Distinguished Reviewer Award. PB members were asked to apply the following criteria: 1. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s111\",\"d1\",27131,27424,\"The reviewer consistently wrote high quality reviews that: ○ Gave clear feedback to authors in terms of soundness, significance, verifiability and presentation ○ Gave clear recommendations for accept/reject decisions ○ Did so in a professional manner using polite and constructive language 2. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s112\",\"d1\",27424,27690,\"The reviewer consistently participated in online discussions in a way that was responsive, constructive, collaborative and clear  32 PC members were nominated to receive an award. Most of these were nominated only once (26) with the others nominated multiple times. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s113\",\"d1\",27690,27943,\"These 6 were all selected to receive an award; in addition, the PC Chairs added 2 names from the remaining list based on a careful reading of all the reviews from the nominees. Hence, 8 members of the PC received a Distinguished Reviewer Award.        \\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s114\",\"d1\",27943,28319,\"3 Discussion on Key Aspects of the Review Process  3.a Double Blind Reviewing  ICSE 2019 followed the “heavy” double-blind review model where names of the authors are not revealed until the final decisions (for accepted papers) or never (for rejected papers). The same review model was used in ICSE 2018.   Checking submissions for double-blind violations can be challenging. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s115\",\"d1\",28319,28528,\"The PC chairs and PB members checked papers for double blind violations. Some papers were desk rejected due to egregious double blind violations such as listing the authors’ names with the title of the paper. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s116\",\"d1\",28528,28897,\"Since double-blind reviewing is rather new to ICSE, we decided not to desk reject minor violations such as listing of grants in the acknowledgements that reveal author identities, providing a link to a repository where the user name of the owner is visible, blacking out names of authors in the pdf file (author names can be seen by copy-pasting the blacked out text). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s117\",\"d1\",28897,29241,\"Future PC chairs may decide to apply the double-blind rule more strictly as the ICSE author community becomes more familiar with the process. It would also be helpful to provide some guidelines about how to prevent some common mistakes in anonymizing papers such as, incorrect anonymization of a web link, a tool repository, a pdf file, etc.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s118\",\"d1\",29241,29577,\"We instructed the reviewers not to actively search for submissions online and in general avoid activities that would deanonymize the paper. In some cases reviewers discovered the author identity for various reasons (they already reviewed an earlier version of the work, or they knew about the research as it was being developed, etc.). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s119\",\"d1\",29577,29908,\"We did not reassign papers to other reviewers in cases where the author identities were inadvertently discovered by the reviewers. Our assessment was that the review process should not be disrupted to achieve perfect anonymity since there will always be cases where the reviewers can make an informed guess who the authors may be. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s120\",\"d1\",29908,30282,\"We think that the costs and benefits of achieving better anonymity of the authors will continue to be an issue that future PC chairs will need to take into account.   We provided a Q&A page to clarify the rules for double blind submissions (which was a revised version of the Q&A page from ICSE 2018). However, we still received many questions about double blind reviewing. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s121\",\"d1\",30282,30640,\"One issue authors struggle with is how to reference their prior work. The answer is to cite the prior work in third person. But this may not be possible if the prior work is not published (for example it could be a dissertation). We advised authors to omit citations to their unpublished work and add such citations in the camera-ready version of the paper. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s122\",\"d1\",30640,30936,\"The important thing to communicate to authors is that the double-blind rule should never be used as a justification for not citing prior published work.  Some authors were concerned about how citing a previously published short paper by them (such as a NIER paper) would influence the reviewers. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s123\",\"d1\",30936,31173,\"Authors believed that if the reviewers knew that the authors of the short paper and the full paper are the same, they would be less concerned about the significance of the contribution (delta) between the short paper and the full paper. \",\"document\",[]]],\"required_scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"},\"rules_completion_required\":false,\"supported_task_outputs\":{},\"failed_batch_reviews\":[{\"id\":\"failed_batch_941f8e422a0527d2cfbb9361\",\"variable\":\"time\",\"query_identity\":\"e6ad7f7e6ab9df22fd3389f9af0cd6e9559ffa610181d5bb0bcf3d36f8fb59da\",\"batch_identity\":\"2f7020888f66fb82542e72fdb2201476954fe38a3871d5cba6805df96ad4cb10\",\"task_id\":\"agreement_details\",\"phase\":\"history\",\"source_ids\":[\"s93\",\"s57\",\"s70\",\"s71\",\"s77\",\"s85\",\"s91\",\"s64\",\"s79\",\"s69\",\"s81\",\"s82\",\"s88\",\"s62\",\"s87\",\"s63\",\"s90\",\"s65\",\"s75\",\"s76\",\"s58\",\"s66\",\"s84\",\"s86\",\"s92\",\"s89\",\"s83\",\"s67\",\"s61\",\"s68\",\"s59\",\"s60\",\"s78\",\"s80\",\"s72\",\"s73\",\"s94\",\"s74\"],\"failed\":[{\"variable\":\"time\",\"id\":\"time_obs_1\",\"feedback\":\"Independent original-source grounding review uncerta